# Summary
Introduced. The commit added WAL durability fields to status and computes `durable_wal_clean_len` by calling `wal.clean_len()` from the status path. That helper performs a full read and decode of the WAL, and status is used in a periodic production loop.
The new `ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::status_snapshot` calls `wal.clean_len()` whenever the WAL-backed log store is healthy. `clean_len()` delegates to `read_records()`, which opens the WAL, reads the entire file into memory, and decodes every frame. This changes `ControlPlaneRaftAuthority::status()` from a cheap in-memory snapshot into a full WAL replay scan. In the multi-node experimental Raft control-plane process, `raft_authority.status()` is called on every lease-scan loop to decide whether the local authority is serving. Since the WAL grows with Raft/control-plane mutations and heartbeats, a peer or local control-plane client that can drive WAL growth can make each status check consume increasing CPU and memory, potentially blocking or crashing the control-plane process. Even without malicious input, a long-running cluster can degrade as the WAL grows.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit `4c4ec2039deceaaac7e55eef249b8e5b7261da38` (`Avoid WAL replay in Raft status`).

The fix removes retained-WAL frame replay from the authority status hot path. `ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::status_snapshot()` now obtains WAL status offsets by reading only the CRC-protected file header and the filesystem file length, instead of calling `wal.clean_len()` and decoding every retained WAL frame. Checkpoint and restart replay paths still validate retained WAL frames normally.

Regression coverage corrupts a middle WAL frame and proves `status_snapshot()` still succeeds without decoding retained frames, while strict WAL replay fails closed on the corruption.

