## Report
Validated the finding. Code review showed the status path now calls the WAL clean-length computation: ControlPlaneRaftAuthority::status maps log_store to ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::status_snapshot at crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:3422-3427; status_snapshot calls wal.clean_len() when WAL-backed and unpoisoned at lines 4196-4202; clean_len delegates to read_records at lines 4890-4891; read_records_from allocates a Vec, file.read_to_end(&mut bytes), then decodes frames in a loop at lines 4919-4974. The production loop calls raft_authority.status() on every multi-node lease scan and sleeps for control_plane_lease_scan_interval at crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2420-2427 and 2467. A debugger trace on the existing status test confirmed the runtime call chain: frame #0 ControlPlaneRaftWalFile::clean_len at control_plane_raft.rs:4891, frame #1 ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::status_snapshot at control_plane_raft.rs:4197, frame #4 ControlPlaneRaftAuthority::status async closure at control_plane_raft.rs:3425. A second lldb trace stopped in read_records_from at line 4919 immediately before read_to_end, with frames clean_len -> read_records -> read_records_from. Dynamic PoC results on valid WAL files showed linear cost: 10,000 valid frames / 710,000 bytes took elapsed_ms=27 with VmHWM_kB=3816; 1,000,000 valid frames / 71,000,000 bytes took elapsed_ms=2720 with VmHWM_kB=110940. A low-memory sparse WAL stress under ulimit -v 131072 on a 512 MiB WAL returned Err(Io { context: read control-plane OpenRaft WAL, source: Kind(OutOfMemory) }), confirming the full-file read can hit memory limits. Direct crash was attempted but Rust read_to_end returned an OOM error instead of SIGABRT in this environment; valgrind was unavailable and ASan required nightly Rust, so debugger plus real API performance measurements were used.

