# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Kept as medium. The code evidence supports the bug: status() reaches status_snapshot(), which calls wal.clean_len(), which fully reads and decodes the WAL. The status path is periodically executed in the multi-node control-plane lease-scan loop, so WAL growth can cause recurring CPU/memory exhaustion and possibly process exit. However, the affected surface is experimental, disabled by default, and exposed through private Unix-domain internal control-plane/Raft interfaces rather than unauthenticated public HTTP. This supports a meaningful availability issue but not high/critical severity.
## Likelihood
low - Exploitation is not reachable from the normal public S3 HTTP surface in the reviewed code. It requires an experimental multi-node Raft deployment plus an internal actor capable of submitting/replicating control-plane activity or otherwise growing the Raft WAL. Those preconditions make broad exploitation unlikely, but the path is plausible for a compromised or malicious internal peer/storage node.
## Impact
medium - The vulnerable path can consume CPU and memory proportional to the entire WAL on every periodic status check. Validation evidence described linear runtime/memory growth and an OOM read error under a memory limit. In the manager loop, a status error exits the control-plane process, so impact can be control-plane availability loss. It does not directly expose data, bypass authentication, or execute code.
## Assumptions
- The affected deployment enables ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT and configures a multi-node Raft peer set.
- A realistic attacker is an internal/control-plane actor: a configured Raft peer, compromised storage node, or local process with access to the private Unix-domain control-plane or Raft peer socket.
- Public S3 HTTP clients are not assumed to have direct access to this Raft status path unless another bug or deployment choice exposes the internal sockets.
- control-plane process role
- ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT=true
- multi-node Raft peer mode with more than one configured peer
- healthy WAL-backed Raft log store
- attacker can cause Raft/control-plane mutations or heartbeats that append WAL records
## Path
Internal peer/storage node
  -> Raft/control-plane command/heartbeat
  -> WAL grows
  -> periodic lease-scan raft_authority.status()
  -> status_snapshot() calls wal.clean_len()
  -> read_to_end() + decode every WAL frame
  -> control-plane slowdown/OOM/error exit
## Path evidence
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:3419-3427` - ControlPlaneRaftAuthority::status() calls ControlPlaneRaftLogStore::status_snapshot(), placing log-store/WAL status computation on the authority status path.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4183-4202` - status_snapshot() calls wal.clean_len() whenever a WAL exists and is not poisoned.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4890-4891` - clean_len() is implemented by reading WAL records instead of using cached metadata.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4919-4974` - read_records_from() reads the entire WAL into a Vec and decodes every frame, creating O(WAL size) memory and CPU cost.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2406-2467` - In multi-node Raft mode the control-plane manager calls raft_authority.status() on each lease-scan loop and exits on status check failure.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:306-330` - Experimental Raft is disabled by default; the lease-scan interval defaults to 250 ms when enabled.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:455-463` - Raft-specific configuration requires ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT, confirming this path is feature-gated/experimental.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2695-2749` - Control-plane Unix socket directories are created/validated as private and owner-only, limiting direct exposure.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:2031-2059` - Raft peer RPC handling applies timeouts and validates incoming peer frame identity before dispatching, but this does not bound WAL-status scanning cost.
- `crates/storage/src/control_plane_raft.rs:4575-4584` - Log-store record application appends records to the WAL when WAL-backed, establishing the WAL growth source.
## Narrative
The finding is real for experimental multi-node Raft deployments. status() obtains a log-store status snapshot; status_snapshot() computes wal_clean_len by calling wal.clean_len(); clean_len delegates to read_records(); read_records_from opens the WAL, read_to_end()s it into memory, then decodes frames in a loop. The argmin-s3 control-plane manager calls raft_authority.status() every lease-scan interval in multi-node Raft mode and exits on status error. WAL records are appended for log-store records, so an internal peer/storage-control actor that can drive Raft/control-plane mutations can make later periodic status checks increasingly expensive. Severity remains medium: impact is meaningful availability loss of the control-plane process, but reachability is limited to an experimental, disabled-by-default, internal Unix-socket/Raft-peer surface rather than unauthenticated public HTTP.
## Controls
- ARGMIN_CONTROL_PLANE_EXPERIMENTAL_RAFT feature/config gate
- Control-plane and Raft peer interfaces use Unix-domain sockets, not public TCP listeners in the reviewed code
- Socket parent directory must be private and owned by the process uid
- Raft peer frames validate configured cluster/node identity
- Peer RPC worker limit and 1-second I/O timeouts
- Default HTTP listener is 127.0.0.1:9000 and is separate from the vulnerable control-plane status path
- No repository IaC/ingress artifact found exposing the control-plane socket publicly
## Blindspots
- Static review only; no deployment manifests or runtime socket permissions were available to verify real-world exposure.
- The provided validation evidence demonstrates resource growth but does not prove public HTTP reachability.
- Actual impact depends on WAL compaction/checkpoint behavior, operator memory limits, and how control-plane unavailability affects a deployed cluster.
- Peer identity validation appears non-cryptographic in the reviewed path; local socket filesystem permissions and deployment topology determine whether untrusted local users can connect.
